Body Text:
In 1642, as war began to look increasingly likely, many castles were being strengthened in preparation for the coming hostilities. Robert Greville of Warwick Castle spent much of the first part of 1642 building up his castle’s defences. He created barricades, artillery mounts and stocked up on gunpowder. Greville supported Parliament. Even before Charles I had formally declared war Warwick Castle was being besieged by Royalist soldiers.  The siege began on 7th August 1642. Greville was away, and had left the castle in the charge of Sir Edward Peyton, who steadfastly refused to surrender it to the Royalists. In response, the Royalists opened fire but didn’t do a great deal of damage to the castle. In the words of a Parliamentarian veteran Richard Bulstrode;

?... we had only two small pieces of cannon which were brought from Compton House, belonging to the Earl of Northampton, and those were drawn up to the top of the church steeple, and were discharged at the castle, to which they could do no hurt, but only frightened them within the castle, who shot into the street, and killed several of our men.?

The siege ended with a Royalist retreat to Worcester, as Robert Devereux approached with a Parliamentarian force. The siege ended on 23rd August, the day after King Charles raised his standard in Nottingham. The castle saw no further military action during the wars, but was frequently used to house prisoners.

Wilne Ferry was a Royalist outpost on the Derbyshire/Leicestershire border. It acted as a vital communication route. The strategic value of such a garrison appealed to Lord Grey of Groby and Sir John Gell who launched an offensive against it in July 1644. Rather than attack the fortress head on though, the Parliamentarians tried a different tactic. A contemporary account described what happened;

?They took about sixty cartloads of hay, and other combustible matter, and carried them with their forces up to the very fort, under the shelter of which their men were secure from any hurt from the enemy; and putting fire to the combustible matter, with the advantage of the wind, did so annoy the enemy that they were driven out of the works.?

The smoke from the fires proved too much for the Royalist defenders, who offered terms for surrender, but they were refused. The Parliamentarian soldiers stormed the castle, and the Cavaliers immediately threw down their weapons and begged for surrender, which was finally accepted. One defender died, and the 80 strong garrison were taken prisoner.
